+- **The cost the model does not charge**: a 4-bit newest counter per 1.25 s holds 15. Over the whole stream (1,305,412 events), events past their unit's cap: HEAD 2,694 (0.21%, 453 of 2,000 keys); every H/48 row 22,110 (1.69%, 805 keys); H/120 rows 69,842 (5.35%, 998 keys). The model counts them exactly, as the filter does here (Capacity 200,000, hot entries free); under hot-entry pressure (floods, design load) those keys' counts would come from the overflow table's floors instead, and the gain shrinks by an amount this simulation cannot read.
+
+**Verdict: worth building** (the rule holds for 4 of the 9 front rows, smallest 50 + 40 bits). Not adopted: this is a design note for the reclaimer the split needs; the bit budget and the cap traffic are your decisions.
+
+**Design note: a guaranteed reclaimer, and what the bits go to.**
+1. *What it must guarantee.* With lastTouch truncated to 32 bits, age = (now − ts) mod 2^32 is right while the true age is under 2^32 ticks (71.6 min at 1 µs; 49.7 days at 1 ms). A reclaimer that visits every line at least once per period P, and expires (frees, or marks stale) every slot whose age exceeds the horizon H, keeps every stored age under H + P; it is enough that H + P < 2^32 ticks. At 1 µs and a 60 s horizon, P can be up to ~70 min; a P of one horizon leaves a margin of 70x.
+2. *Where it runs.* The overflow table's advance and decay already sweep on a schedule (N7 measures their per-call work); the reclaimer can ride the same incremental sweep, a bounded number of lines per touch or per Background tick, with a progress word that wraps once per P. Its proof obligation is the one §8.3 names: no line is skipped for a full P (a stalled sweep must force a catch-up before any slot's age can pass 2^32 − margin), so a TLA+ model beside Relocate.tla (sweep vs relocation vs install) comes before code.
+3. *Word layout* (filter.go: word0 = fp 16 | displaced | saturated | lastTouch 46; word1 = tag 12 | epoch 4 | register 48, no spare bit). With lastTouch at 32 bits word0 has 14 free bits, and word1 has none, so a wider register in word1 (one CAS per touch, as today) needs bits moved out of word1: the tag moves into word0 (fp 16 + tag 12 + 2 flags + lastTouch 32 = 62 bits, 2 free), which frees 12 bits of word1. Then a register of up to 60 bits fits word1 beside the epoch, and whatever word1 bits the register leaves go to identity: 50 + 40 = word0's 2 free bits + word1's 10 (identity 16 + 12 + 2 + 10 = 40); 58 + 32 = 2 + 2. Splits past 60 register bits would also need the epoch moved, so the 62-bit rows don't fit this shape. The cost: a slot's identity no longer straddles the two words the way the slot protocol (Relocate, SlotHold, TagStripe models) assumes; matching on word0 alone would be cheaper, but the models must be redone first. The register geometry itself is one table change in register.go (`geomUnits` 48, steps 1 1 2 4 8 16 16 16, `bucketBits0` 4, eight buckets).
+4. *Before building, read:* (a) the 50-bit geometry in the filter under tfbench's flood rows and the h2h floods (does the 8x cap traffic exhaust hot entries?); (b) the reclaimer's work per call with N7's counters; (c) the FPR at 40 identity bits makes static false positives unmeasurable at any trace size (3e-12): the 12 spare identity bits could instead go to the 1 s window (D19's side information, the last events' offsets), which this experiment did not score.
+
+## N5: page-local alternate lines (S, deterministic part only)
+
+Status: done. **Not adopted**: no arm meets the rule.
+
+**Background read first** (Vacuum filters, Wang et al., PVLDB 13(2), 2019, §3.3–3.4): the table is cut into chunks of L consecutive buckets (L a power of two, table size a multiple of L) and Alt(B, f) = B ⊕ (H′(f) mod L), so both buckets of an item lie in one chunk. L is chosen by a balls-into-bins test: with m buckets of 4 slots, N = 4·r·m·α items over c = m/L chunks must have EstimatedMaxLoad(N, c) = N/c + 1.5·√(2·(N/c)·ln c) < 0.97 × 4L (Algorithm 1); RangeSelection(n, α, r) is the smallest such L (Algorithm 2). The final design (Algorithm 3) splits items into four groups by fingerprint bits with L[i] = RangeSelection(n, 0.95, 1 − i/4), and doubles the smallest, L[3].
+
+**What is new.** Clepsydra's alt(l) = (x(fp) − l) mod lines (`slots.go`) spans the table, so a key's two lines, and each step of a cuckoo search, are usually different 4 KiB pages. No experiment has changed the alternate-line function.
+
+**Arms** (one patch, `go/verify/experiments/next-n5-local-alt.patch`; the arm is chosen by `TF_ALT` at New, unset = HEAD's function, so the gates must be identical with it unset):
+- `TF_ALT=64`: chunks of 64 lines (4 KiB of table). For line l in chunk c: base = c·L, size = L, i = l − base, x = ⌊mix(fp)·size / 2^64⌋ (HEAD's x, on the chunk), alt = base + (x − i) mod size: an involution within the chunk, any table size. Clepsydra's tables are not a multiple of L, so the last full chunk takes the remainder (size L + (lines mod L)): every line keeps a chunk of at least L lines and alt stays its own inverse. (Read literally, "the last partial chunk uses the full range" would make alt not an involution for keys whose alternate falls in it — this is the reading that keeps relocation correct; noted as a deviation.) A table under L lines is one chunk (HEAD's function).
+- `TF_ALT=32768`: the same with L = 32,768 lines (2 MiB).
+- `TF_ALT=vacuum`: four groups by the fingerprint's low two bits; group i uses L_i = RangeSelection(lines × 4 × 0.95 / 4… i.e. m = lines, α = 0.95, r = 1 − i/4) with 4 slots per line, L_3 doubled, L ≥ lines meaning the whole table (HEAD's function). For tfbench's table (Capacity 200,000, 58,824 lines) that is [whole table, 128, 32, 16] lines: a quarter of the keys keep HEAD's range, the rest get 8 KiB, 2 KiB and 1 KiB chunks. α = 0.95 as the paper's Algorithm 3 (the test cannot pass at 0.978 for r = 1: N/c alone is 0.978 × 4L > 0.97 × 4L).
+
+**Measurements.**
+- `TF_ALT=<arm> go run ./cmd/tfbench -exact` (patched tree): the three churn rows (load 1.00 / 1.10 / 1.15 = ~85 / 94 / 97.8% slot load: missed, evictions, relocations, false_seen) and static_fpr (1e9 probes).
+- Pages per cuckoo search: `TF_EXP=1 go test -run '^TestLocalAltPages$' -v .` (in the patch): replays tfbench's churn stream (internal/workload, seeds 2/3/4, the same three loads) through CheckAndTouchAt with a test hook in claimAt and relocate; a "search" is one entry into claimAt's relocation phase (both candidate lines full), and its pages are the distinct 4 KiB groups of table lines (line >> 6, table-relative: the table is 64-byte aligned, so true page boundaries may shift by a constant) among the two candidate lines and every alternate line relocate computes. Mean, p50 and p99 per load, and the number of searches.
+- Gates with `TF_ALT` unset (the patch only adds the arm switch and a nil-checked hook).
+
+**Rule** (the prompt's, made exact before reading). An arm is a candidate for a bare-metal speed reading (not judged here) if all hold: (1) missed = 0 in all three churn rows; (2) evictions at load 1.15 ≤ 1.10 × HEAD's 4,577 = 5,034; (3) static_fpr within Poisson noise of HEAD's 18 in 1e9: the arm's false positives in 11..28 (HEAD's printed 95% interval, 1.07e-8–2.84e-8); (4) mean distinct pages per cuckoo search at least 4x lower than HEAD's at each of the three loads. Also reported: relocations, false_seen, the gates.
+
+**Readings.** Patched tree (`git apply --directory=go go/verify/experiments/next-n5-local-alt.patch`). Gates with `TF_ALT` unset: identical (sanity 56 s, digest 66 s, exact 19 s; a first run failed only on gofmt, fixed). `TestAltChunkInvolution` passes (alt is its own inverse for every arm on table sizes 2–58,824, multiples of L and not). `TF_ALT=<arm> tfbench -exact` and `TF_ALT=<arm> TF_EXP=1 go test -run '^TestLocalAltPages$' -v .`; raw: `lab/readings/n5-*.txt`.
+
+| arm | load | missed | evictions | relocations | false_seen | searches | pages/search mean | p50 | p99 | HEAD / arm (mean) |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| HEAD | 1.00 | 0 | 0 | 148,716 | 0 | 142,922 | 5.013 | 4 | 14 | |
+| HEAD | 1.10 | 0 | 0 | 379,688 | 0 | 291,711 | 9.446 | 7 | 38 | |
+| HEAD | 1.15 | 0 | 4,577 | 932,499 | 0 | 416,794 | 59.647 | 33 | 301 | |
+| L = 64 | 1.00 | 0 | 1,851 | 176,671 | 0 | 153,325 | 1.001 | 1 | 1 | 5.0x |
+| L = 64 | 1.10 | **1** | 31,563 | 391,262 | 0 | 291,898 | 1.001 | 1 | 1 | 9.4x |
+| L = 64 | 1.15 | **54** | **78,519** | 500,552 | 0 | 374,378 | 1.001 | 1 | 1 | 59.6x |
+| L = 32,768 | all | identical to HEAD (every row and page count) | | | | | | | | 1.0x |
+| vacuum [whole, 128, 32, 16] | 1.00 | 0 | 0 | 158,288 | 0 | 147,519 | 2.498 | 2 | 7 | 2.0x |
