+## N9: a per-key "spilled" bit (L)
+
+Status: done. **Not adopted**: rules (2) and (3) fail; (1) and (4) hold.
+
+**What is new.** Under floods every touch of a key reads its overflow-table buckets once the table holds histories (47% of CPU at 16x, S64; N7: heavy16x reads 1.99 buckets per call), though most keys never spilled. Lab E4's per-line hint failed because every line sees spills in a flood; S1, S2/S2d, S66/S67/S71 made the read cheaper, not rarer. Here the read becomes rarer: a bit per key.
+
+**Design** (patch `go/verify/experiments/next-n9-spilled-bit.patch`):
+- word0 takes one bit from lastTouch: `spillBit` = bit 45; lastTouch 45 bits (`tsMask` = 2^45 − 1, so MaxTick halves: ~1.1 years at 1 µs; the move D16 made for the saturated bit; the bit budget is your decision, this measures what it buys).
+- **Set at install**: a first touch (touch's miss path) installs the key with the bit set if `recall` found a stash or wait-room history, or the overflow table — read *before* the install, under the key's claim stripe — returns anything for the key (an entry matching its fingerprint, or a live floor covering it); and always under ConstantTime. The first touch's answer uses that same read (HEAD read it after the install). A wait-room entry (Background) carries the bit in its word0 to the placer's install; a move copies word0 whole; `Merge` installs a key only g holds with the bit set if f has an overflow table.
+- **Read**: touch (hit path), `count` and `ContainsAt` skip the overflow read when the key was found (table, stash or wait room) with the bit clear and ConstantTime is off. A key not found reads it as today.
+- **The race with a concurrent spill** (the prompt's sketch: spill writes the overflow entry before freeing the stash way; a first touch re-checks after install when a spill ran meanwhile). Read from the code before writing the model: HEAD already orders both halves. (a) An eviction holds the victim's claim stripe from taking its slot through `stashPut`, including a direct spill when there is no stash (cuckoo.go claimAt), and a first touch takes its own stripe before `recall`; (b) `stashPutAs` spills an outgoing history to the overflow table *before* `stashWrite` overwrites its way, both under the stash set's lock, which `recall` also takes. So a history that leaves the table is, at every instant a first touch can look, in the table, the stash or the overflow table. The patch therefore adds no re-check; the model below decides whether that is enough (if it is not, a re-check counted like moves is added and the model re-run, before any measurement).
+- **SpilledBit.tla** (`go/verify/tla/SpilledBit.tla`, run by `verify/tla.sh SpilledBit`), written before measuring: key K with a slot, an evictor (K's slot to M, K's history to the stash), a stasher (another history into K's stash set, which pushes K's out to the overflow table), K's toucher (touches; a miss is a first touch: stripe, recall, overflow check, install with the bit) and a reader (reads count the slot, plus the overflow table only if the bit is set or K was not found). Invariants: **ReadNotLow** (a read's count ≥ the events of K that completed before it began) and **TouchNotLow** (a touch's answer counts every earlier event). Variants: "fixed" must hold; each guard undone must violate: **evictNoStripe** (the eviction releases K's stripe between taking the slot and stashing), **wayFirst** (the stasher frees the way before the spill writes the overflow table, the set lock released between), **checkOutsideStripe** (the first touch's overflow check before taking the stripe and recall), **noBit** (the bit is never set: reads always skip).
+
+**Measurements and rule** (the prompt's):
+1. `./test.sh sanity` and `go test -race -short -count=1 .` pass except TestConformance's vectors and TestMemoryOnFirstUse (expected to change with word0's layout; named, each failure shown); the attack check (`./test.sh regression`'s attacks, diff against verify/expected/attacks.txt: any difference explained), the soak (`TF_STRESS=60s TestStressSoak`, plain and `TF_BG=1`) and the fuzzers (`TF_FUZZ=1e8 TestFuzzUpper`, `TF_CFUZZ=2m TestFuzzConcurrent`; the default 1e9 / 5m do not fit a foreground command here: stated) pass; `verify/replay.sh stress 300` clean except TestAbandonedMoveKeepsCounts' documented one-high residual.
+2. heavy16x and heavy4x t1 ≥ 1.10x HEAD's in at least 5 of 6 pairs each (`lab/flood.sh`).
+3. Library-only Ir (`lab/cg.sh`, n = 50000/150000): TouchOverflowLive and CountOverflowLive ≥ 30% lower; CheckAndTouchHit and InsertSteady ≤ +1%.
+4. Strict false denies non-inferior by lab E6b's rule: heavy1x, heavy2x, heavy4x, 10 `acc` rounds per arm alternated (order reversed on even rounds), `h2h falsedeny`; the upper end of the paired 95% t-interval of mean(N9 − HEAD) ≤ +20 of 40,000 on each.
+Also: which digest cells and `tfbench -exact` rows differ, and why; SpilledBit.tla's runs.
+
+**SpilledBit.tla** (`go/verify/tla/SpilledBit.tla`, `go/verify/tla.sh SpilledBit`, 8 s): a first version violated ReadNotLow in the "fixed" variant: a read found K nowhere while K's first touch held its history between `recall` (which takes the stash entry) and the install. That is HEAD's protocol, not the bit: `read` (touch.go), after a stable lock-free miss with the stash gate up (`stashMay`), takes the key's claim stripe and looks in the table and the stash again; the model's reader did not. With the reader modeled as `read` is, the runs:
+
+| run | result | states |
+|---|---|---|
+| fixed, Touches = 2, Reads = 1 | ok (want ok) | 7,694 |
+| fixed, Touches = 1, Reads = 2 | ok (want ok) | 6,206 |
+| evictNoStripe | TouchNotLow violated (want violation) | 786 |
+| wayFirst | TouchNotLow violated (want violation) | 2,340 |
+| checkOutsideStripe | TouchNotLow violated (want violation) | 3,288 |
+| noBit | ReadNotLow violated (want violation) | 5,071 |
+
+So the existing orderings close the race the prompt describes (an eviction under the victim's stripe; the spill's overflow write before its way is freed, under the set lock) as long as the first touch's overflow read comes after `recall`, under its stripe; no re-check is added. (evictNoStripe and wayFirst break HEAD's own first-touch answer too; checkOutsideStripe and noBit are the bit's own guards.)
+
+**Patch and rule (1)** (`go/verify/experiments/next-n9-spilled-bit.patch`: slots.go, filter.go, touch.go, cuckoo.go, merge.go; the model and `tla.sh`'s runner are committed under `go/verify/`).
+
+- *A defect found by the soak, fixed before any speed reading:* the first version installed the bit on every install path but one: `miss`'s fallback when a Background key's wait-room set is full (`claim(..., sat, claimFull)`, cuckoo.go). `TF_BG=1 TestStressSoak` caught it: 1,294,498 unflagged answers with Upper below the true count (a key's first event in the overflow table, its second in a fresh slot with the bit clear). Fixed (`sat|sp`); every rule-(1) check below was run on the fixed patch (sanity, gates and race were run again).
+- Gates: `./test.sh sanity` **pass** (45 s; TestConformance and TestMemoryOnFirstUse pass too); `tfbench -exact` **identical**; the answer digest differs in **13 of 30 cells** (`lab/readings/n9-digest.txt`), all `repeat=6000` cells, the saturated workloads where the overflow table is live: plain seeds 1 and 3 (spill1=false), bg seeds 2 and 3 (spill1=false), and all six spill1=true cells, plain and bg, and the three cms=true cells. Fields that differ: count, the count batches, touch, check, contains. Why: a key whose bit is clear no longer adds the overflow table's matches to its answer — other keys' entries that share its overflow fingerprint, and floors — so Count, Upper, Seen and Saturated drop to the key's own slot; and a first touch's answer is read before its install instead of after, so a history its own install evicted no longer reaches it. ConstantTime cells are unchanged (the bit is always set there).
+- `go test -race -short -count=1 .` **ok** (151 s).
+- `./test.sh regression`: every check passes except the answer digest (above); **attacks identical** to verify/expected/attacks.txt.
+- Soak, `TF_STRESS=60s TestStressSoak`: plain **pass** (143 M ops, 42.9 M counts checked, 0 low behind a live floor, lost 0); `TF_BG=1` **pass** (127.7 M ops, 38.3 M counts, 0 low).
+- Fuzzers at their defaults (they fit after all; the design's 1e8/2m were not needed): `TF_FUZZ=1e9 TestFuzzUpper` **pass** (1,008,741,208 ops; 344,550 low behind a live floor and 3,081,064 low after a loss with the overflow off, the fuzzer's tolerated classes; HEAD on the same seed: 344,548 and 3,081,064, so +2 in a class of 344,548; Saturated flags 44.93 M vs HEAD's 45.71 M); `TF_CFUZZ=5m TestFuzzConcurrent` **pass** (755.9 M ops). A 1e8 / 2m run before them also passed.
+- `verify/replay.sh stress 300`: **0/300** failures in each of the four racy tests (5 m 19 s; not even the documented residual).
+- SpilledBit.tla: above.
+
+Rule (1): **holds.**
+
+**Rule (3), cachegrind** (`lab/cgm.sh lab/readings/n9-cg.txt head9=HEAD n9=HEAD+…next-n9-spilled-bit.patch -- …`; library-only Ir/op, n = 50000/150000):
+
+| benchmark | HEAD | N9 | change | needs |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| TouchOverflowLive | 5,517.95 | 5,503.77 | −0.26% | ≤ −30% **fails** |
+| CountOverflowLive | 1,575.14 | 1,736.41 | **+10.24%** | ≤ −30% **fails** |
+| CheckAndTouchHit | 502.83 | 500.82 | −0.40% | ≤ +1% holds |
+| InsertSteady | 1,742.86 | 1,761.73 | **+1.08%** | ≤ +1% **fails** |
+
+Why the overflow benchmarks don't move: they flood a Capacity-2^18 filter with 8 × its slots of keys (4 touches each), then first-touch the 32,768 keys they measure. A scratch test on that state (not committed): 23,094 of the measured keys are in the table, and **99.1% have the bit set**; of the 32,768 keys' overflow answers over the horizon, **32,406 come from floors alone** (no matching entry). Among the flood's own keys in the table, 98.6% have it. Once the overflow table has overflowed, live floors cover nearly every bucket, and a floor may hold the key's own dropped history, so the bit must be set (SpilledBit's guard; the prompt's own trigger): the bit skips nothing. CountOverflowLive's +10% and InsertSteady's +1.08% were not traced further (no overflow read is added on either path; the restructured first-touch path and the different table the setup builds are the candidates).
+
+**Rule (2), floods** (`lab/flood.sh HEAD HEAD+…next-n9-spilled-bit.patch <flood>`, 6 pairs; `lab/readings/n9-flood.txt`). A first reading compared N9 against an h2h binary cached under the name "HEAD" in Batch 0, built from cedeefd (before main merged S5/S4b/S3/S7): void, discarded (it read heavy16x 1.05–1.28). `lab/flood.sh` and `lab/fd.sh` now key their binaries by commit. The valid reading, HEAD = 0832dbd (main 2ca617b's code):
+
+| flood | B/A per pair | ≥ 1.10 |
+|---|---|---|
+| heavy16x | 1.001, 1.098, 0.869, 0.972, 1.046, 0.965 | **0 of 6: fails** |
+| heavy4x | 1.256, 1.387, 1.497, 1.482, 1.086, 1.353 | 5 of 6: holds |
+
+At 4x the overflow table holds fewer floors, so most keys keep the bit clear and touches skip the read: 1.09–1.50x, the largest flood gain this lab has read. At 16x the table is past its own capacity and floors cover everything, as in the benchmarks.
+
+**Rule (4), strict false denies** (`lab/fd.sh HEAD HEAD+…next-n9-spilled-bit.patch <flood>`, 10 `acc` rounds per arm alternated; of 40,000; `lab/readings/n9-fd.txt`), mean(N9 − HEAD) [95% paired t]: heavy1x +0.20 [−1.30, +1.70]; heavy2x −6.90 [−14.72, +0.92]; heavy4x +0.70 [−14.69, +16.09]. Upper ends ≤ +20 on all three: **holds** (heavy2x leans fewer denies: unspilled keys no longer pick up other keys' floors).
+
+**Verdict: not adopted** (rules 2 and 3 fail). What it shows: the bit is correct (TLA+, soak, fuzzers, stress, false denies) and fast where the overflow table is live but not saturated (heavy4x +9–50%), and useless once floors cover the table, which is where S64 measured the cost (16x). A variant worth a rule of its own: floors that record which keys they absorbed (e.g. a per-bucket "floor epoch" compared with the key's install time, so a floor raised before a key's install, by other keys, doesn't set its bit), or a bit set only by matching entries with floors excluded for keys whose own history never left the table; neither is read here.
