+## N12: fine entries for short windows, simulated (M)
+
+Status: done. **Not worth building** at any size the rule allows.
+
+**Question.** Target 7's gap is the 1 s window (0.2138); D19 read that the exact times of a key's last 8 events give 0.041; §8.3 proposes wider state for the keys whose short windows matter, like hot entries; memory leaves ~1.2 B/key at design load. Here a side table of 64-byte **fine entries** is modeled (no library change).
+
+**Model** (`TF_EXP=1 go test -run '^TestFineEntries$' -v .`, a test file beside velinfo_test.go; patch `go/verify/experiments/next-n12-fine-entries.patch`):
+- Workload and score: tfbench's velocity workload (`velWorkload`, 2,000 keys, 240,000 queries over 1/5/15/30/60 s), normalized MAE per window and their mean, as tfbench and N4b. Base estimate: HEAD's register (the N4b model, equal to the filter).
+- **Table size, a choice stated before reading:** the velocity workload runs 2,000 keys in a Capacity-200,000 filter, so 1/128 of *that* filter's slots (1,838 entries) would hold nearly every key. Sizes are therefore taken against a filter built for the workload's own keys (Capacity 2,000 at 85% load = 2,353 slots): **1/128 = 18 entries, 1/64 = 37, 1/32 = 74**, i.e. +0.58, +1.18, +2.37 B/key (64 B × entries / 2,000 keys). One more arm, every key with an entry from its first event, bounds what the entry can do at all.
+- A fine entry holds what 64 bytes can: the exact ticks of the key's latest 8 events and a finer register (unit H/240 = 250 ms; bucket steps 1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 64, 64, 64 units, register.go's aligned rule; counters uncapped), both fed only by events from its promotion on. A query with window W is answered from the entry only if the entry has existed for at least W (its promotion tick ≤ now − W): the last 8 exact, the rest by the finer register's interpolation (`simEstimate` with `known`); otherwise from the base register.
+- Promotion (a key without an entry gets one; a full table evicts one), three policies: **(a)** a query with W under 2 units (W < 5 s: the 1 s queries) promotes the queried key; victim the least recently touched entry; **(b)** a touch that leaves ≥ 3 events in the base register's newest unit promotes the key; victim the least recently touched entry; **(c)** as (a), victim the entry least recently queried with a short window.
+
+**Output.** Pareto: per (size, policy) MAE per window and mean against B/key; promotions per second.
+
+**Rule** (the prompt's). Report the Pareto; "worth building" if some size at ≤ +1.2 B/key (18 or 37 entries) cuts the 1 s error by ≥ 30% (to ≤ 0.1497) with no window worse than HEAD's (5 s 0.0567, 15 s 0.0513, 30 s 0.0436, 60 s 0.0240: each not higher).
+
+**Readings.** `git apply --directory=go go/verify/experiments/next-n12-fine-entries.patch; cd go; TF_EXP=1 go test -count=1 -run '^TestFineEntries$' -v .` (4 s; `lab/readings/n12.txt`):
+
+| arm | B/key | 1 s | 5 s | 15 s | 30 s | 60 s | mean | promotions/s |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| HEAD | — | 0.2138 | 0.0567 | 0.0513 | 0.0436 | 0.0240 | 0.0779 | — |
+| every key (bound) | (64 B × keys) | **0.0177** | 0.0487 | 0.0498 | 0.0346 | 0.0185 | **0.0339** | 8.3 |
+| 18 entries, (a) / (b) / (c) | +0.58 | 0.2138 / 0.2138 / 0.2138 | 0.0567 | 0.0513 | 0.0436 | 0.0240 | 0.0779 | 198.5 / 4,102.7 / 198.5 |
+| 37 entries, (a) / (b) / (c) | +1.18 | 0.2137 / 0.2138 / 0.2138 | 0.0567 | 0.0513 | 0.0436 | 0.0240 | 0.0779 | 196.6 / 3,894.5 / 196.7 |
+| 74 entries, (a) / (b) / (c) | +2.37 | 0.2132 / 0.2138 / 0.2138 | 0.0567 | 0.0513 | 0.0436 | 0.0240 | 0.0778 | 193.2 / 3,588.1 / 193.0 |
+
+By the rule: no size at ≤ +1.2 B/key moves the 1 s error (best 0.2137, needs ≤ 0.1497): **fails**. The Pareto has two points: the sized tables (no gain) and the every-key bound.
+
+Why: an entry answers a W-window query only once it has existed for W, and on this workload the table churns. Queries pick keys uniformly (each key's next 1 s query comes ~10 s later), so query-driven promotion (a, c) evicts the entry long before it is asked again (18–74 entries at ~200 promotions/s live 0.1–0.4 s); burst-driven promotion (b) qualifies most keys (≥ 3 events per 2.5 s unit is a rate of 1.2/s) and turns the table over ~4,000 times a second. The entry's information is worth a lot — with an entry from each key's first event the 1 s error falls 92% (0.2138 → 0.0177) and every window improves (the D19 picture, now with all windows) — but it needs the key to keep its entry, i.e. a table near the hot key count, not 1–4% of slots. On a workload with locality (a few keys queried on short windows, repeatedly) a small table would behave like the bound for those keys; this workload has none, and N13's MAWI reader could supply one.
+
+**Verdict: not worth building** (as sized by the rule). The bound is worth recording: 64 B of exact recent ticks per key would meet target 7's 0.05 at 1 s with 3x margin.
