+## N17: black-box co-location attack (M; report only)
+
+Status: done (report only).
+
+**Threat** (after Reviriego and Ting, "Breaking Cuckoo Hash: Black Box Attacks", 2022): no ConstantTime, keyed hashing (the attacker doesn't know `Config.Key`); the attacker sends its own keys and times each request, looking for keys that share a victim's two lines, then floods them. §6 lists "hash-flood one region" as needing the key (row 7); this asks whether timing replaces the key.
+
+**Two parts.**
+1. *The oracle* (analysis, from readings already taken). A request's time follows its work, which N7 counts: an unseen insert at 85% load costs 4 line reads with room in its lines and up to 86 (p99 32) with a cuckoo search, and 44% of first touches find both lines full (S38). That tells the attacker whether *its own* key's two lines were full, not which lines they are; and a victim's lines are touched only by the victim's requests, which the attacker doesn't see. So the black-box oracle does not single out a victim's lines; how far the attacker's own timing narrows them down is not measured here (wall clock can't resolve it on this VM: b = 0.41).
+2. *The damage, given the oracle anyway* (upper bound; `TF_EXP=1 go test -run '^TestColocation$' -v .`, patch `go/verify/experiments/next-n17-colocation.patch`): the test plays a perfect co-location oracle (it computes lines with the filter's own hash). Capacity 200,000, 1 ms ticks, 60 s horizon, defaults otherwise; 200,000 background keys each touched once a minute (the table at its design load); 100 victims each touched once a second (60 per window). **Probes needed**: per victim, the attacker draws random keys until 16 have a candidate line equal to one of the victim's two (the count of draws is what a perfect oracle would still need: the attacker must try keys to find co-located ones). **Flood**: from 90 s to 150 s each of the 1,600 co-located keys is touched 10 times a second (16,000 events/s); **control**: 1,600 random keys at the same rate. Every 100 ms from 90 s to 180 s each victim is read (`CountAt`, W = 60 s): Upper below its true count unflagged (not Saturated); strict false denies with a limit of 100 (denied by Count ≥ 100, Saturated or Upper ≥ 100 while the truth is under 100, as h2h's strict recipe); Degraded share; and evictions of victims (a victim not found in the table).
+
+**Rule:** report only. Compared: targeted vs control on the same victims (the control is §6.5's kind of flood, untargeted).
+
+**Readings.** Patched tree; `TF_EXP=1 go test -count=1 -run '^TestColocation$' -v .` (3 s; `lab/readings/n17.txt`). 100 victims × 901 reads (every 100 ms, 90–180 s) = 90,100 reads per arm; 1,600 attack keys, 16,000 events/s for 60 s.
+
+| arm | probes | Upper < truth unflagged | strict false denies | Degraded | victim outside the table | evictions | folded |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| one line (as designed: a candidate line equal to one of the victim's) | 24,895,039 (15,559 per key) | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+| control (random keys) | — | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+| **both lines** (added after the first two readings: keys built with the victim's fingerprint and primary or alternate line) | ≈ 1.93 × 10⁹ per key expected (2¹⁶ × lines / 2) | **0** | **0** | 0 | 52,300 of 90,100 reads | 612,938 | 0 |
+
+Reading it (report only):
+- *Oracle* (part 1): the attacker's own request times say whether its key's two lines were full (N7, S38), not which lines; this host can't time it anyway (b = 0.41).
+- *One shared line does nothing.* Even with a perfect co-location oracle (the test's own hash), 16 keys sharing one of each victim's lines, flooded at 10/s each, caused **0 evictions**: the cuckoo search moves entries out through their other line. A key co-located on one line costs ~15,600 random probes (expected lines / 4 = 14,706: a key matches if either of its two lines is either of the victim's two).
+- *Both lines* is what it takes: the key's alternate line is a function of its fingerprint, so a key whose two lines are the victim's two needs the victim's 16-bit fingerprint and one of its lines: ≈ 1.9 × 10⁹ random probes per key at this size (58,824 lines), and the attacker must also recognise one when it has it. Given 16 such keys per victim anyway (built directly), the victims were evicted constantly (58% of reads found the victim outside the table) — and every answer stayed fail-closed and allowed: **0 unflagged Upper below the truth, 0 strict false denies, 0 Degraded**, because each evicted victim's history sat in the stash (folded 0) and came back on its next touch. With a smaller stash or a heavier flood those histories would go to the overflow table, the regime of §6.4's "Repeatedly floored keys"; not read here.
+- Against §6.5's untargeted floods: the control here (the same volume, random keys) did nothing to the victims either; the targeted flood's only effect was the evictions.
