+## N19: DecayedRate(h, τ) (S)
+
+Status: done. **Not adopted**: τ = 60 s fails, as stated before reading; 5 and 15 s pass.
+
+**API** (patch `go/verify/experiments/next-n19-decayed.patch`): `func (f *Filter) DecayedRateAt(h uint64, tau float64, now uint64) float64`: Σ over the key's events of exp(−(now − t)/τ) (τ in ticks), from the 7 buckets, no new bits (forward decay's quantity, Cormode et al., ICDE 2009). Each bucket's events are spread uniformly over its span, so a bucket [lo, hi) with c events contributes c · τ/(hi − lo) · (e^{−(now−hi)/τ} − e^{−(now−lo)/τ}); the newest non-empty bucket ends at the key's last touch, whose event counts whole (estimate's convention). The key is read where `count` reads it (table, stash or wait room); the overflow table and hot entries' extra width are not used (a hot key's view is read like a register's buckets). Events older than the register's oldest bucket are invisible to it.
+
+**Measurement** (`TF_EXP=1 go test -run '^TestDecayedRate$' -v .`, in the patch): tfbench's velocity workload (2,000 keys, its 240,000 queries), each query's τ drawn from {1, 5, 15, 60} s (seeded); normalized MAE = Σ|est − exact| / Σ exact per τ, where **exact = the true EWMA over all the key's events so far** (judged), and also against the EWMA truncated to the last horizon (reported, to separate the register's coverage from its bucket error).
+
+**Rule** (the prompt's). Candidate if the normalized MAE against the true EWMA is ≤ 0.05 for τ = 5, 15 and 60 s. Stated before reading: for τ = 60 s, events older than the ~60–80 s the register covers hold e^{−1} ≈ 37% of a steady key's decayed mass, so that τ is expected to fail by coverage alone; the truncated comparison shows the rest.
+
+**Readings.** Patched tree; `TF_EXP=1 go test -count=1 -run '^TestDecayedRate$' -v .` (2 s; `lab/readings/n19.txt`); ~60,000 queries per τ. Gates identical.
+
+| τ | vs the true EWMA (judged) | vs the EWMA of the last horizon |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 s | 0.1128 | 0.1128 |
+| 5 s | **0.0282** | 0.0282 |
+| 15 s | **0.0236** | 0.0253 |
+| 60 s | **0.2001** | 0.0944 |
+
+By the rule (≤ 0.05 for τ = 5, 15, 60 s): 5 s and 15 s hold; **60 s fails**. **Verdict: not adopted** as specified; what it shows: the 7 buckets give an exponentially decayed rate within 3% for τ from 5 to 15 s with no new state (a decayed rate is smoother than a window count, so the cut bucket costs less: 0.028 at 5 s against the 5 s window count's 0.057); 1 s has the register's short-window error (0.11); and a τ comparable to the horizon needs history the register doesn't keep (half of the 60 s error is coverage, half is the coarse oldest buckets). An API restricted to τ ≤ H/4 would pass this rule's spirit; that is your call, and it would be a rule written after this reading.
+
+## N20: trace-fitted cut-bucket estimator (M)
+
+Status: done. **Not adopted**: worse on both held-out sets.
+
+**Question.** estimate (register.go) counts the bucket the window's edge cuts by a uniform spread of its events (E8's medians and the piecewise-linear density used the same uniform model). Here the cut fraction is learned, conditioned on the register's pattern, and judged only on held-out traffic.
+
+**Model** (the register as N4b models it: HEAD's geometry, uncapped counters, equal to the filter; the answer is estimate's interpolation with the cut bucket's fraction replaced). The correction table T has one cell per context: the cut bucket's index j (0–6), its count class (1, 2–3, 4–7, 8–15, 16+), the window start's position within the bucket (8 equal bins), and whether the next newer bucket is empty (2) — 560 cells; a cell holds Σ (the cut bucket's events inside the window) / Σ (its events) over the training queries in that context (the newest event, at the last touch, excluded as estimate excludes it); a cell with fewer than 50 training queries falls back to the uniform fraction.
+
+**Data.**
+- *Training*: the MAWI trace's first half (N13's trace and reader, extended: `lab/mawi -records`: for each uniform query (b), the key's model register built from its exact packet times — kept two horizons back — with the query's window, now and exact count; queries before the trace's midpoint train, after it are held out) **plus** synthetic shapes: tfbench's velocity workload generator with seed 3 (not tfbench's 9).
+- *Held out*: the MAWI trace's second half, and tfbench's velocity workload (seed 9).
+- Score per held-out set: normalized MAE per window (1, 5, 15, 30, 60 s) and their mean, uniform vs fitted.
+
+`TF_EXP=1 N20_MAWI=<records> go test -run '^TestFittedCut$' -v .` (patch `go/verify/experiments/next-n20-fitted-cut.patch`).
+
+**Rule** (the prompt's). Candidate if the mean MAE falls by ≥ 0.003 on **both** held-out sets, with no window more than 5% worse on either.
+
+**Readings.** `lab/mawi -records <file> <trace>` (7 m, both passes: the reader's (a) and (b) lines repeat N13's exactly; 840,014 records, 96 B each; `lab/readings/n20-mawi-reader.txt`); `TF_EXP=1 N20_MAWI=<file> go test -count=1 -run '^TestFittedCut$' -v .` (`lab/readings/n20.txt`). Training: 420,006 MAWI queries (first half) + the velocity generator at seed 3; 391 of 560 cells fitted (≥ 50 queries). The model with the uniform fraction reproduces HEAD on tfbench's workload (0.2138, 0.0567, 0.0513, 0.0436, 0.0240), so it is the filter's estimate.
+
+| set | uniform → fitted: 1 s | 5 s | 15 s | 30 s | 60 s | mean |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| MAWI second half (held out) | 0.2288 → 0.2335 | 0.0221 → 0.0344 | 0.0506 → 0.0610 | 0.0339 → 0.0460 | 0.0108 → 0.0213 | 0.0693 → **0.0793** |
+| velocity, seed 9 (held out) | 0.2138 → 0.2250 | 0.0567 → 0.0609 | 0.0513 → 0.0577 | 0.0436 → 0.0496 | 0.0240 → 0.0266 | 0.0779 → **0.0840** |
+| MAWI first half (training) | 0.3177 → 0.3201 | 0.0446 → 0.0530 | 0.0286 → 0.0356 | 0.0130 → 0.0198 | 0.0163 → 0.0236 | 0.0841 → 0.0904 |
+
+By the rule (mean −0.003 on both held-out sets, no window 5% worse): **fails** — worse in every window of every set, the training set included. Why: a cell replaces the uniform fraction with one constant over its eighth of the bucket, so it throws away the interpolation within the bin; the information it adds (where events sit, by context) is smaller than what it discards. **Verdict: not adopted.** (A note on the reader: in records mode it keeps two horizons of packet times per source and builds each model register without bump's reset after an idle horizon, which applies equally to both arms.)
